PAGES=[page('080_page_2.jpg',184,[heading('7.2 Slope of a Straight Line'),paragraph('The slope of a line is a measure of the when “steepness” of the line, and whether it rises, or falls when moving from left to right. The line from A to B rises up, while the line from C to D goes down are depicted in the Figure 7.6:'),graph(6,'steepness'),heading('7.2.1 Slope of a line'),paragraph('The graph of a line can be drawn knowing only one point on the line if the “steepness” of the line is known, too.'),paragraph('“A number that measure the ‘steepness’ of a line is called slope of a line.”'),graph(7,'rise-run'),paragraph('If we move off the line horizontally to the right or move up or down (vertically) to return to the line, then the slope of the line is the “steepness” defined as the ratio of the vertical rise to the horizontal run:'),eq(r'\mathrm{slope}=\frac{\mathrm{rise}}{\mathrm{run}}'),paragraph('the run is always a movement to the right'),heading('7.2.2 Formula to find the slope of a line passing through two points'),paragraph('Mathematically, if any two points on a line are available, then their join makes a constant angle with a fixed direction and the angle so formed is independent of the choice of the two points on the line. This is a precise way of saying that any line has a constant slope. It is customary to measure the angle θ which a line makes with the positive direction of the x-axis. The quantity tan θ is defined to be the slope of the line and is denoted by m. The slope of a line is also referred to gradient of the line.'),paragraph('For illustration, if A(x₁,y₁) and B(x₂,y₂), where x₁ ≠ x₂, are any two points, then their join develops a line L that makes a constant angle θ with the x-axis. Draw AM, and BN parallel to y-axis and AL parallel to x-axis.'),graph(8,'two-points'),paragraph('The slope m of a line L through the two points A(x₁,y₁) and B(x₂,y₂), is therefore:'),eq(r'm=\tan\theta=\frac{LB}{AL}=\frac{NB-NL}{MN}=\frac{NB-AM}{ON-OM}=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}','i'),heading('Example 6'),paragraph('Find the slope m of the line L through the points (a) E(2,4) and F(4,6). (b) M(3,1) and N(−1,3).'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('a. The given two points E(2,4) and F(4,6) form a line L, whose slope is:'),eq(r'm=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\frac{6-4}{4-2}=\frac22=1'),paragraph('b. The given two points M(3,1) and N(−1,3) form a line L, whose slope is:'),eq(r'm=\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}=\frac{3-1}{-1-3}=\frac2{-4}=-\frac12'),heading('Remember'),paragraph('The standard equation of a line is y = mx+c where m is a slope.'),note('Rise is signed: moving down gives a negative rise and a negative slope. The finite slope and y = mx+c form exclude vertical lines, whose equation is x = constant. The source repeats Figure 7.6 after using that number for the preceding incenter figure; both source captions are preserved.')]),page('081_page_1.jpg',185,[heading('7.2.3 Condition for two straight lines with given slopes are'),paragraph('(a) Parallel to each other (b) Perpendicular to each other'),heading('a. Parallel to each other'),paragraph('If L₁ and L₂ are the two lines having slopes m₁ and m₂, then the lines L₁ and L₂ are parallel if they make the same angle with the x-axis, that means they have the same slope. Conversely, if two lines L₁ and L₂ have the same slope, then they will make the same angle with the x-axis and the lines L₁ and L₂ are therefore parallel for which:'),eq(r'm_1=m_2','i'),paragraph('It is important to note that the lines parallel to x-axis have zero slopes whereas the lines parallel to y-axis have the slope ∞.'),heading('b. Perpendicular to each other'),paragraph('If L₁ and L₂ are the two perpendicular lines make the angles α and β with the x-axis, then the slopes of the lines L₁ and L₂ are respectively m₁ = tan α and m₂ = tan β. From the Figure 7.9, it is clear that'),graph(9,'perpendicular'),eq(r'\frac\pi2=\beta-\alpha\ \Rightarrow\ \beta=\frac\pi2+\alpha'),eq(r'\tan\beta=\tan(\frac\pi2+\alpha),\quad\mathrm{take\ tan\ of\ both\ sides}'),eq(r'=-\cot\alpha=-\frac1{\tan\alpha}','ii'),paragraph('The given lines L₁ and L₂ are found perpendicular, since the product of their slopes equals −1:'),eq(r'm_1m_2=\tan\alpha\tan\beta=\tan\alpha\left(-\frac1{\tan\alpha}\right)=-1','iii'),note('Equal finite slopes give the same direction; two distinct lines are parallel, and coincident lines also share a slope. A vertical line has undefined finite slope; ∞ is the source’s shorthand. The product criterion m₁m₂ = −1 applies when both slopes are finite. A horizontal line is perpendicular to a vertical line, which needs this separate case.'),heading('7.3 Equation of a Straight Line Parallel to Co-Ordinate Axes'),heading('7.3.1 Equation of a straight line parallel to'),paragraph('○ y-axis and at a distance ‘a’ from it. ○ x-axis and at a distance ‘b’ from it.'),heading('i. y-axis and at a distance ‘a’ from it'),paragraph('Let PQ be a straight line parallel to y-axis at a distance ‘a’ units from it see Figure 7.10. This is very clear that all the points on the line PQ have the same ordinate say ‘b’. Therefore, PQ can be considered as the locus of a point at a distance ‘a’ from y-axis and all points on the PQ satisfy the condition x = a. Therefore, the equation of a straight line is parallel to y-axis at a distance ‘a’ from it. e.g. x = a.'),graph(10,'vertical'),heading('Remember'),paragraph('i. If a = 0, then the straight line coincides with the y-axis and its equation becomes x = 0.'),paragraph('ii. If PQ is parallel and to the left of y-axis at a distance ‘a’, then, its equation is x = −b.'),note('For the vertical line the fixed coordinate is its abscissa x = a, not the “ordinate b” printed in the paragraph. At distance a > 0 to the left of the y-axis, its equation is x = −a; the source’s final −b is a variable mismatch. Distance a alone allows two sides, x = ±a; Figure 7.10 depicts the right side.')])]
